  "headline": "Armies Without Governments",
  "regional_lead": "Russia fired an Oreshnik ballistic missile at the Kyiv region on the night of May 23-24, part of one of the war's heaviest bombardments since the 2022 invasion. The same night, Marco Rubio, the US secretary of state, was in Helsingborg for a NATO foreign ministers' meeting, where he announced that US troop numbers in Europe would be cut. The message was plain: as Russia deploys its most powerful operational weapon, Washington is stepping back. The question for Central-Eastern Europe is whether its governments can fill the gap. The week suggested most of them cannot.\n\nUkraine is the exception — and even Ukraine is showing the strain. General Oleksandr Syrskyi told the Ukraine-NATO Council that Ukrainian offensive actions now outnumber Russian ones, the first such reversal he has reported. Ukrainian forces have recaptured roughly 400 sq km since winter, struck 11 Russian oil facilities in May, and are deploying ground robots and private air-defence companies at a pace most European NATO members have not matched. Yet even as Kyiv builds this capacity, Volodymyr Zelensky picked a fight with Germany — writing to EU presidents to call Friedrich Merz's associate-membership proposal \"unfair,\" the German chancellor having suggested Ukraine attend EU meetings without voting rights as an interim step — while intelligence showing Russian forces preparing five offensive scenarios through the Chernihiv-Kyiv corridor forced him to order reinforcements north. Andrii Sybiha, the foreign minister, declared US-facilitated peace talks \"gradually reaching a point of exhaustion\" and called for more European involvement. Ukraine is doing more than any other state in the region to match the moment. The strain of doing so is showing.\n\nRomania showed the gap between military seriousness and political capacity most starkly. On May 19, a Romanian F-16 pilot fired a single missile over Estonia and downed a drone — the first time a NATO jet has engaged a target over alliance territory. The aircraft was hundreds of miles from Romanian soil; Mark Rutte, the NATO secretary-general, praised Romania for changing the relevant legislation in \"a day or two.\" At home, Romania cannot sign its own defence contracts. The Finance Ministry has not approved commitment credits for over €8 billion in equipment purchases; without a minister with full spending authority, those contracts may lapse. The country has had no functioning government since a no-confidence vote on May 5. The central bank has raised its year-end inflation forecast from 3.9% to 5.5%, with a peak of around 11% projected for July. Mugur Isărescu, the central bank governor, said plainly that \"it will not be good if the formation of the new government takes a long time.\" Romania's pilot proved the country can act. The state around him cannot.\n\nPoland illustrates a different kind of hollowing out: informal channels replacing institutions. Before the American president posted on Truth Social announcing 5,000 additional troops to Poland, Karol Nawrocki, the country's cohabitation president, had already sent him messages through a direct number. Donald Tusk, whose government had spent the previous week defending its American relationship, had no choice but to say thank you. But the announcement was not coordinated with the NATO command. General Alexus Grynkewich, who commands US and NATO forces in Europe, confirmed that 5,000 troops were \"coming out of Europe,\" with an armoured brigade from Poland among them. US defence officials said they did not understand the announcement's implications. A senior politician close to the episode was blunter: \"We hang on Trump alone.\" In the Czech Republic the damage runs from within. NATO foreign ministers named Sweden, Germany, Denmark, Norway and others as \"heavy lifters\" on Ukraine support; the Czech Republic was not among them. Andrej Babiš has maintained he will contribute \"not one crown\" from the state budget to Ukraine arms procurement, while his outgoing army chief used his final international platform to call Ukraine's NATO membership \"the logical next step\" — a direct contradiction of his own government. Mr Babiš is also trying to sell Explosia, a state-owned maker of Semtex and military explosives, to France — while French prosecutors investigate him over his Côte d'Azur villa.\n\nHungary offers the region's only genuine countermovement, and it is instructive about pace. Two weeks into office, Péter Magyar, Hungary's new prime minister, submitted a constitutional amendment that would bar Viktor Orbán from returning as prime minister and begin reclaiming state assets channelled into loyalist foundations. His first foreign trip was to Warsaw, where Mr Tusk offered access to American liquefied natural gas through the Gdańsk terminal — a concrete mechanism with a date, 2028, for a country drawing 85% of its gas from Russia. These are real changes. But the prosecutor general remains silent, the government has not moved to join the European Public Prosecutor's Office, and the state broadcasting architecture stands unchanged. Mr Magyar acknowledged it himself: \"This will take years.\" The region may not have years. In Helsingborg, NATO foreign ministers debated a model in which Europe takes full responsibility for conventional defence, with the United States providing only nuclear cover. For that to work, European governments must be functional as well as willing. Several of the most exposed are neither.",
  "gap_paragraphs": [
    "Notably absent this week: CEE NATO members coordinating European conventional defense capacity to compensate for US retrenchment signaling. The gap is structurally significant. All four NATO members in the region received the same US retrenchment signal in the same week, and the expected response — coordinated European defense capacity building at the regional level, as the structural framework predicts should be active given Helsingborg NATO 3.0 framing — did not materialize as a cross-CEE dynamic. The most plausible explanation is that the Nawrocki-Trump personal channel gave Poland a nominally differentiated outcome that reduced its immediate incentive to invest in the European coordination track, while Czech Republic's cohabitation prevents a coherent contribution, Hungary is in its first two weeks of government and cannot yet contribute institutionally, and Romania is incapacitated by its formation crisis. The coordination gap may be temporary (structural incentives remain strong) but the week's evidence shows national channels are being pursued before regional ones.",
    "Notably absent this week: Ukrainian diplomatic pivot toward European-led peace framework producing visible European CEE response. Ukraine has now officially declared the US peace-facilitation track approaching exhaustion and called for European leadership. The regional framework predicts this should activate visible Polish, Romanian, or Czech diplomatic positioning, given that all three are frontline or near-frontline NATO members with high stated investment in Ukraine's outcome. The absence of documented response in any country report either reflects a collection gap (European diplomatic back-channel activity not captured in open-source monitoring), or that CEE governments are waiting for larger European actors (Germany, France, UK) to take the lead before committing to a framework. Given the week's domestic preoccupations in each country — Poland's swatting incident and troop announcement volatility, Romania's formation crisis, Czech Republic's coalition friction, Hungary's transitional institutional actions — the domestic absorption may account for the absence.",
    "Notably absent this week: Belarus threat escalation producing documented coordinated CEE response through NATO channels. Poland is the NATO member most directly exposed to a Belarus-origin Russian offensive threat (Suwalki Gap, direct border with Belarus). The expected dynamic — Polish security planning response to a credible northern threat that Zelensky has ordered reinforcements to address — does not appear in the PL security_defense assessment this week, which focuses entirely on the Trump troop announcement and Grynkewich withdrawal. This is either a genuine absence (Poland's planning response to the northern threat is not reflected in open-source this week) or a collection gap. Given that Syrskyi's NATO Brussels briefing covered this material and Poland's defense establishment would have access, the absence from Poland's security_defense assessment is worth flagging for the country desk."
  ],
  "card_summary": "Eastern Europe's soldiers can fight, but the states behind them are too broken to arm themselves or fill the gap America is leaving."
